<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mukden Incident</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> Incident was a staged explosion on a Japanese-owned railway near Mukden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931. Officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> used it as a pretext to occupy Manchuria without authorisation from Tokyo. The incident led to the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> and showed the army's growing power over the government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mukden-incident.jpg" alt="Mukden Incident" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mukden_1931_japan_shenyang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s economic and political interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> had grown since the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904 to 1905. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-portsmouth">Treaty of Portsmouth</a> gave Japan the lease of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-manchuria-railway">South Manchuria Railway</a>, and Japanese railway guards were stationed along it, though they often carried out manoeuvres outside the railway zone. Meanwhile the newly formed Chinese government sought to reassert its authority and challenged treaties with Japan. The Manchurian warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a> was assassinated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a>, and his son Zhang Xueliang joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>. In 1929 the Soviet Red Army defeated Zhang Xueliang's forces in a conflict over the Chinese Eastern Railway, which revealed Chinese military weakness to Kwantung Army officers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Colonel Seishirō Itagaki and Lieutenant Colonel Kanji Ishiwara planned to provoke an incident in order to prompt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. When the Minister of War sent Major General Yoshitsugu Tatekawa to curb the army's insubordination, they decided to stage the incident themselves. They chose a spot near Liutiao Lake, only eight hundred metres from the Chinese garrison at Beidaying. At about 10:20 p.m. on 18 September 1931, Lieutenant Suemori Kawamoto of the unit guarding the railway set off explosives beside the track. The blast damaged only a 1.5 metre section of one rail, and a train passed over it within minutes.</p><p>The army blamed Chinese dissidents and on 19 September opened fire on the Chinese garrison near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a>. Five hundred Japanese troops attacked a garrison of around seven thousand, and by evening Mukden was occupied at the cost of five hundred Chinese lives and only two Japanese dead. General Shigeru Honjō, the commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a>, at first objected that the plan had been carried out without his permission but then approved it. Zhang Xueliang had ordered his men not to resist. Within five months the Japanese army had overrun all the major towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> appealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, which on 24 October demanded Japanese withdrawal, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> refused. In March 1932 Japan established the puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> as head of state. The Lytton Report of 2 October 1932 rejected the claim that the invasion was self-defence, but did not say that the Japanese had set off the explosion. The League refused to recognise Manchukuo, and Japan left it in March 1933.</p><p>Strong evidence points to young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> officers having plotted the blast, with or without orders from Tokyo. Historians differ over whether the Tokyo leadership shared their aims, the historian James Crowley arguing that the officers and the civilian leaders held the same overall intentions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mukden incident” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 1930s, factions within the army were conducting independent foreign policy, staging provocations such as the <b>Mukden Incident</b> of September 1931, while young officers assassinated civilian leaders, including Prime Minister Inukai Tsuyoshi in May 1932, and attempted a coup in the February 26 Incident of 1936.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The garrison, renamed the Kwantung Army in 1919, acted with growing independence and in 1931 staged the <b>Mukden Incident</b>, leading to the creation of Manchukuo.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-leased-territory">Kwantung Leased Territory</a></li></ul></section>
